artist biography | definition | An artist biography is the record of a career: where somebody trained, what they made, what changed and when. I can explain the subject and how the field is put together, and I can be accurate about work that is finished and settled, but I cannot tell you what a living artist released this month or what a current chart says. There is no live source behind this bank, so a name typed on its own is likely to be one I do not have, and I will say so rather than describe somebody's work I have not got. | artist biography, biography, career, training, work, live | how do i find out about an artist, tell me about this singer, do you know any celebrities | artist biography | biography ng artista

# helpful answer turns into a wrong one.

artist | definition | An artist is somebody who makes work judged by something other than its function, and the word covers visual art, music, performance and writing without separating them cleanly. What unites the uses is that the making and the reception are the point, rather than a building being erected or a problem being solved. It is also a word that flatters, since calling something an art rather than a craft implies judgement that may not be there. | artist, art, work, craft, function, judgement | what is an artist, is art a career, difference between art and craft | artist | artista
painting | definition | Painting is pigment applied to a surface, and the oldest surviving painted surfaces are cave walls thousands of years old, which shows the medium came first and the galleries came much later. Paint changes behaviour as it ages, which is why a painting is a record of materials as much as of decisions; oil keeps moving for centuries and continues to crack. Reproductions cannot fix that, since what is reproduced is an image rather than the substance, and that is the ongoing argument in museums. | painting, pigment, surface, canvas, oil, age | what is painting, how does painting work, is oil paint better than acrylic | painting | pagpupainting
portrait | definition | A portrait is a representation of a person, and it is a negotiation rather than a record, since the sitter chooses how they are seen and the painter decides what to emphasise. A likeness and a good portrait are not the same goal: a likeness can be accurate and lifeless, and a good portrait tells you what the person is like. Photography changed the commercial half of the business almost completely, while the commissioned single portrait survives where status is the point. | portrait, sitter, likeness, face, representation, status | what is a portrait, is a portrait a likeness, why are portraits painted | portrait | larawan
famous painter | definition | The painters most often named are the ones whose work was reproduced enough to escape their own century, and that is a selection effect rather than a judgement of quality. The usual names cluster in a few periods because patrons funded them then, not because those centuries produced more talent. Most had ordinary lives and were unknown outside their city, and the canon was assembled much later by museums, dealers and textbooks. | famous painter, artist, canvas, museum, patron, period | who are the famous painters, why are some painters famous, is fame deserved | famous painter | sikat na pintor
musician | definition | A musician is somebody who plays or composes, and the work splits into performance and composition in ways that used to be one job and now are rarely. The economics differ too, since performing income is unreliable and peaks early while composing income arrives late and can continue for decades. The shift that mattered in the last century was the recording, which separated the audience from the room and made it possible to be paid repeatedly for the same hour. | musician, play, compose, performance, recording, audience | what is a musician, is being a musician a good career, do musicians make money | musician | musikero
stage name | definition | A stage name is the name somebody performs under, and it is chosen far more often now than earlier performers could afford to do, when it was expensive to be a stranger. The reasons are now partly practical, since a name that is easy to say, spell and search changes how much money the work finds. It is also a signal that the person is being treated as a product, which is what a performer is both willing and ambivalent about. | stage name, perform, name, search, product, privacy | what is a stage name, why do singers use stage names, is a stage name a pseudonym | stage name | pangalan sa entablamen
